===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪŋˈkɹoʊt͡ʃmənt/|/ɛŋˈkɹoʊt͡ʃmənt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-encroachment.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# An [[entry]] into a place or area that was previously uncommon; an [[advance]] beyond former borders; [[intrusion]]; [[incursion]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1949|month=November and December|title=Notes and News: Festiniog and Welsh Highland Railways|journal=Railway Magazine|page=408|text=At the same time, the '''encroachment''' of vegetation proceeds apace, and broom and brambles have already made portions of the line impassable, even on foot.}}
# An [[intrusion]] upon another's [[possession]]s or [[right]]s; [[infringement]].
#* {{RQ:Federalist|number=XXXXVII|passage=The Legislative department derives a superiority in our Governments from other circumstances. Its constitutional powers being at once more extensive, and less susceptible of precise limits, it can, with the greater facility, mask, under complicated and indirect measures, the '''encroachments''' which it makes on the coördinate departments.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|volume=I|chapter=V|pages=53–54|pageref=54|passage=Ah! my brother, we do well to watch our birthright jealously; the least invasion on the meanest peasant, the slightest '''encroachment''' of the powerful, are not matters to be neglected—such are the first steps of tyranny.}}
# That which is [[gain]]ed by such [[unlawful]] intrusion.
# {{lb|en|law}} An [[unlawful]] [[diminution]] of the [[possession]]s of another.

